Binary search uses ascending order to discard half of the candidate interval.

The iterative search takes O(log n) worst-case time and O(1) auxiliary space.

If a matching element exists, its index stays within the inclusive interval [low, high].

Set low=mid+1 or high=mid-1 to shrink the interval; low+(high-low)/2 avoids sum overflow. Stop when low>high.

Test [1,3,5], target 3 -> 1; target 4 -> -1.

Test [], target 2 -> -1; [2], target 2 -> 0; [2,2], target 2 -> either 0 or 1.

static int binarySearch(int[] values, int target) {
    int low = 0, high = values.length - 1;
    while (low <= high) {
        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
        if (values[mid] == target) return mid;
        if (values[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;
        else high = mid - 1;
    }
    return -1;
}

The shown Java method returns -1 after the loop; an empty array starts with high=-1, so no element is accessed.

