import java.util.Arrays;

final class CountingSortSuggestion {
    static int[] countingSort(int[] values) {
        int max = Arrays.stream(values).max().getAsInt();
        int[] count = new int[max + 1];
        for (int value : values) count[value]++;
        int[] out = new int[values.length];
        int index = 0;
        for (int value = 0; value < count.length; value++)
            while (count[value]-- > 0) out[index++] = value;
        return out;
    }
}

samples.answerText:
Counting Sort records each value's frequency and reconstructs ascending values, repeating each value by its frequency.

With k=max-min+1, this implementation takes O(n+k) time and O(n+k) total extra storage including the returned array.

On empty input, max().getAsInt() fails. Indexing counts by a negative value also fails; offsets from min are required.

Compute (long)max-min+1 before allocating; reject a width above 1,000,000 so integer overflow and excessive allocation cannot bypass the bound.

Test [] -> []; [2,-1,2,-1,0] -> [-1,-1,0,2,2].

[0,999999] must be accepted; [0,1000000] must be rejected. [Integer.MIN_VALUE,Integer.MAX_VALUE] must be rejected without overflow or a large allocation.

static int[] countingSort(int[] values) {
    if (values.length == 0) return new int[0];
    int min = values[0], max = values[0];
    for (int value : values) { min = Math.min(min, value); max = Math.max(max, value); }
    long width = (long) max - min + 1;
    if (width > 1_000_000L) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Range too wide");
    int[] counts = new int[(int) width];
    for (int value : values) counts[(int) ((long) value - min)]++;
    int[] result = new int[values.length];
    int index = 0;
    for (int offset = 0; offset < counts.length; offset++) {
        for (int repeat = 0; repeat < counts[offset]; repeat++) result[index++] = min + offset;
    }
    return result;
}

In the shown code, width is long, its limit is checked before new int[(int)width], and value-min uses long arithmetic before casting.

